## Why This Is Asked
A practical, low‑level fairness mechanism relevant to high‑throughput systems.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic operations and memory ordering
- FIFO fairness and starvation avoidance
- Cache alignment and false sharing
- Busy-wait semantics and platform hints

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  alignas(64) atomic_uint64_t next;
  alignas(64) atomic_uint64_t grant;
} ticket_lock;

void lock_init(ticket_lock *l){
  atomic_init(&l->next, 0);
  atomic_init(&l->grant, 0);
}
uint64_t lock_acquire(ticket_lock *l){
  uint64_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&l->next, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&l->grant, memory_order_acquire) != t) {
    // pause hint
  }
  return t;
}
void lock_release(ticket_lock *l, uint64_t t){
  atomic_store_explicit(&l->grant, t+1, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses understanding of thread-local caches, lock-free fallbacks, memory alignment, and ABA problem mitigation in a practical systems programming context.

## Key Concepts
- **Thread-local storage (TLS)**: Provides low-latency allocation by keeping memory local to each thread, avoiding contention
- **Lock-free data structures**: Use compare-and-swap (CAS) operations to safely manipulate shared state without locks
- **Memory alignment**: Ensures 128-byte aligned blocks; cache line alignment prevents false sharing between threads
- **ABA avoidance**: Tagged or generational pointers track state changes to prevent the ABA problem in lock-free algorithms

